# Line endings are decided here rather than by each contributor's core.autocrlf.
#
# The index is already uniformly LF - 911 text files, not one CRLF - so this
# records the state the repository is in rather than changing it. Adding it costs
# no renormalisation and buys the guarantee that a second machine, a CI runner or
# an agent with different Git settings cannot start writing the other kind.
#
# It exists because files kept coming back as CRLF on disk and Git then listed
# them as modified with an empty diff. Telling that apart from a real edit is
# cheap when one person is working and expensive when two are, which is exactly
# when it happened: `as_driven_db/site.py` and `tests/test_site.py` both showed
# up as changed while being byte-identical to HEAD.
* text=auto

# Windows-only scripts. PowerShell tolerates LF, but CRLF is the convention for
# both, and cmd.exe is the one interpreter here that can genuinely misread a
# multi-line construct without it.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The only binaries in the tree: the README and docs captures. Declared so no
# filter can touch them and no diff tries to render them as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
